UniXNU (github.com/Droid-VM/UniXNU) 2e61915, drivers/NativeRamBlock, BSD-2-Clause-Patent (LICENSE). Vendored 2026-11-11 so the
native build reads no driver source from outside this repository.

UniXNU-piano changes (NativeRamBlock.cpp), formerly applied at build time by tooling/k27/build-native-ram-block.sh:
- publishWhenBSDMatched (the change macOS-DroidVM's scripts/build-native-ram-block.py applied): the disk is published from a
  thread once IOResources has matched IOBSD (APFS deadlocks otherwise); then it waits for NativeUfs's root layout
  (native_ufs_store_ready), opens the store and only then publishes the disk.
- The six native_ufs_store_* hooks are plain imports (the KC linker takes no weak imports): NativeUfs provides them (data-persist=2).
  The upstream virtio data store is not part of the native build.
- attachStore logs "NativeRamBlock: Data store state ...".

UniXNU-piano changes of 2026-11-11 (docs/data-store.md):
- The store guard (PersistOverlay.h open -> Open::Kept, state 7): a valid store made for another seed is left untouched and the disk
  runs volatile for that boot, unless data-store-replace=1 (or =0x<the store's 8 hex>) or sheng-data-reset=1|2 asks to start over.
  Upstream re-initialised it ("stale"), which dropped every Data change whenever a RAM image brought another seed.
- VolatileDisk.h: a large seed (above 2 GiB) without the store open read-write has a volatile write path (pages in RAM); upstream
  refused its writes (kIOReturnNotReady). The read-only probe (data-persist=1) of a large disk no longer dereferences sparse.pages.
- Self-contained stores (header extension "SHNGEXT1" in bytes 64..127 of block 0, flag kSelfContained) and Overlay::flatten
  (data-store-flatten=1): every seed page written into the store, then the flag; DirectDisk.h then reads clear bits as zero, so the
  store works with any seed of its size. The version 1 header (bytes 0..63) is unchanged, so earlier kernels still read the store.
- Test/: upstream's host tests sparse.cpp, persist.cpp (its stale-seed case now expects Kept, then Fresh with replace), direct.cpp,
  seeddisk.cpp, seed-identity.cpp (sparse.cpp: the over-size case moved past SparseDisk.h's 1 TiB limit); new guard.cpp and
  volatile.cpp; run-tests.sh.
- native-ram-trace=1 (2026-11-11, docs/forensics-2026-11-11.md): a console line before and after each step of the store's set-up
  (the waits for IOBSD and NativeUfs's root layout, the GPT check, the window, the header and bitmap I/O, the syncs, the disk's
  registration), with the milliseconds since the publish thread started. Off without the argument; the set-up's I/O is unchanged.
- The Data disk's part of a system sleep (2026-11-11, NativeUfs's native-ufs-suspend=1, docs/drivers/ufs.md §12): PowerHold.h
  (new) and in NativeRamBlock.cpp joinPower / setPowerState: with the store open read-write the disk is NativeUfs's power child
  (native_ufs_power_parent); off syncs the store under the gate and holds new requests (doAsyncReadWrite waits), a failed sync
  vetoes NativeUfs's suspend (native_ufs_power_veto); on releases them. Without the argument (or with Data volatile) no part, as
  before. Test/power.cpp (new): PowerHold.h on a store with a volatile write cache and power cuts.
- The volatile disk's memory (2026-11-12, after three data-persist=0 runs hung the tablet under Spotlight's first import): VolatilePool.h
  (new). Pages come from 2 MiB IOMallocAligned chunks that a refill thread allocates without the gate; the I/O path only takes ready
  pages after admitting its need (volatileAdmit): refused with kIOReturnNoSpace past the budget (data-volatile-mib, default a quarter
  of memory), kIOReturnNoMemory after 2 s of waiting with the gate released. The first VolatileDisk allocated zone pages
  (IOMallocZero) inside the gate and could deadlock with pageout. Unmap (doUnmap, IOStorageFeatures Unmap, volatile only,
  data-volatile-unmap=0 off) gives APFS's freed blocks back. The status line "NativeRamBlock: volatile: N pages, M MiB resident ..."
  from the refill thread, rate-limited. VolatileDisk.h: volatileNeed, volatileUnmap. Test/volatile-stress.cpp (new).
- The boot reservation and the fallbacks (2026-11-12, the first desktop session: at 1080 MiB held the 2 MiB IOMallocAligned failed
  87474 times, 13 writes were refused with no memory, APFS read their blocks back as zeros: container corruption). VolatilePool.h:
  chunk records (base, pages, allocator kind) that the refill thread grows; prefillPages taken at start (data-volatile-reserve-mib,
  default the whole budget); fallback sizes 2 MiB, 512, 128, 32 KiB, a page, over three allocators (IOMallocData: the private
  data range; IOMallocAligned: the shared data range, the GPU's; IOMalloc: the default heap); a reserve of 64 MiB that doubles
  after a wait up to 256 MiB; after a round with nothing, a backoff of 10 ms doubling to 1 s on an event the writers do not signal
  (the old one was cut short by every waiting writer: a busy loop). NativeRamBlock.cpp: a refused or short volatile write completes
  with kIOReturnIOError (was kIOReturnNoSpace / kIOReturnNoMemory); the admit deadline 10 s (was 2 s); the status line also from the
  backoff, with ready MiB, the reserve and the fallback counts; a line when the reservation is complete. Test/volatile-pool.cpp
  (new): the incident's model, fragmentation, the backoff, bursts, unmap and the reads after it.
